## Why This Is Asked
This question tests reasoning about three-valued logical systems in constraint satisfaction problems, particularly handling partial truth values and implementing efficient propagation mechanisms.

## Key Concepts
- Łukasiewicz three-valued logic: domain {0, 0.5, 1} with specific semantic definitions for implication, negation, disjunction, and conjunction
- Forward-chaining propagation in multi-valued constraint systems
- Bound propagation and domain pruning for partial assignments
- Constraint satisfaction with unknown/indeterminate values

## Code Example
```javascript
function lukasiewiczImplication(a, b) {
    return Math.min(1, 1 - a + b);
}

function lukasiewiczNegation(x) {
    return 1 - x;
}

function lukasiewiczDisjunction(x, y) {
    return Math.min(1, x + y);
}

function lukasiewiczConjunction(x, y) {
    return Math.max(0, x + y - 1);
}

function forwardChainPropagation(variables, constraints) {
    let changed = true;
    while (changed) {
        changed = false;
        for (const constraint of constraints) {
            const result = evaluateConstraint(constraint, variables);
            if (result === 0) {
                return false; // Contradiction found
            }
        }
        // Apply domain pruning based on constraint satisfaction
        changed = pruneDomains(variables, constraints);
    }
    return true;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of least fixed-point computation in monotone boolean networks with thresholds and how to implement an efficient propagation algorithm that converges even with cycles.

## Key Concepts
- Monotone operators and least fixed points
- Threshold-based activation with in-neighbor counting
- Queue-driven BFS-like propagation
- Handling cycles via convergence to a fixpoint

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-implementation of the propagation
function leastFixedPoint(T, edges, thresh) {
  const n = thresh.length;
  const succ = Array.from({length: n}, () => []);
  for (const [u,v] of edges) succ[u].push(v);
  const count = Array(n).fill(0);
  const inSet = Array(n).fill(false);
  const q = [];
  for (const i of T) { inSet[i] = true; q.push(i); }
  while (q.length) {
    const u = q.shift();
    for (const v of succ[u]) {
      count[v]++;
      if (!inSet[v] && count[v] >= thresh[v]) {
        inSet[v] = true;
        q.push(v);
      }
    }
  }
  return Array.from({length: n}, (_,i) => i).filter(i => inSet[i]);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for weighted thresholds or non-monotone edges?
- How would you prove the result is the least fixed point containing T?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests time-expanded 3-valued reasoning and propagation strategies under Kleene semantics. It also checks ability to craft a concrete witness and reason about propagation order across time.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene 3-valued logic; time-expanded CSP; forward propagation; A→B = ¬A ∨ B; monotone reasoning; time-dimension constraints; backtracking with preference for U.

## Code Example
```javascript
function imply(a,b){
  const notA = a==='T'?'F': a==='F'?'T':'U';
  const order = { 'F':0, 'U':1, 'T':2 };
  return order[notA] > order[b] ? notA : b;
}
```

